layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | A
    strong job
    | is a Claude Code run that Buildyboy starts for you on one of your machines: you pick a prompt, a machine, a folder
    | and a budget, and the machine's
    a(href="/docs/runner") runner
    | starts
    code claude -p
    | there. You can launch jobs from your phone, follow their output live, send follow-up messages, stop them, rerun
    | them, check their claims, and run them on a schedule.
  +callout(variant="info")
    @content
      p
        | Jobs need the runner on the target machine. Without it, a job waits in the queue until the runner comes online.
        | Set it up once with
        a(href="/docs/runner") Setting up the runner
        | .

  h2#launch Launching a job
  p
    | Open
    strong Jobs
    | and press
    strong New job
    | , or press
    strong Launch as a job
    | in the
    a(href="/docs/composer") Composer
    | . The
    strong Launch a job
    | form has four parts.
  include "partials/shot.faqir" (assets=site.assets file="jobs-launch-desktop.png" alt="The Launch a job form: a Saved prompt dropdown, a Prompt text box, an optional Title, then Machine and Project directory dropdowns." caption="The top of the Launch a job form.")
  h3#launch-what 1. What to run
  dl
    dt
      strong Saved prompt
    dd
      | A prompt from your library. Choose
      strong — Paste a prompt instead —
      | to type or paste one into
      strong Prompt text
      | .
    dt
      strong Title (optional)
    dd How the job is listed. It defaults to the prompt's title, or its Goal.
  h3#launch-where 2. Where to run it
  dl
    dt
      strong Machine
    dd
      | Each machine is listed with
      code runner online
      | or
      code runner offline
      | . A job for an offline runner waits in the queue ("This machine's runner is offline; the job will wait in the
      | queue.").
    dt
      strong Project directory
    dd
      | Known projects on that machine, recent folders, the runner's allowed roots, or
      strong Other path…
      | to type one in
      strong Directory path
      | . It must be inside one of the runner's allowed roots, or the job fails as soon as it reaches the machine.
    dt
      strong New git worktree (-w)
    dd Runs the job in a separate git worktree, so it does not touch your checked-out files.
    dt
      strong Walk mode
    dd Turns walk mode on for the job's session, so its permission prompts and questions come to your Inbox.
  h3#launch-limits 3. Limits and options
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Field
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Default
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") What it does
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Max budget USD (required)
          td(data-part="td") 5
          td(data-part="td") Claude stops when the run has cost this much. Up to 1000.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Limit (minutes)
          td(data-part="td") no limit
          td(data-part="td") The runner stops the job after this long ("limit of 30 min reached"). Up to 7 days.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Model
          td(data-part="td") Claude Code's default
          td(data-part="td") For example opus, sonnet, haiku or a full model name.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Fallback model
          td(data-part="td") none
          td(data-part="td") Used if the main model is overloaded.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Effort
          td(data-part="td") Default
          td(data-part="td") How hard the model thinks: low, medium, high, xhigh or max.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Permission mode
          td(data-part="td") Default
          td(data-part="td") What Claude may do without asking. See below.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Max turns
          td(data-part="td") no limit
          td(data-part="td") Stops after this many back-and-forth turns.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Extra allowed tools
          td(data-part="td") none
          td(data-part="td") One per line, for example Bash(npm test:*), to pre-approve specific tools.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Disallowed tools
          td(data-part="td") none
          td(data-part="td") One per line; Claude may never use these.
  h3#permission-modes Choosing a permission mode
  p
    | An unattended run cannot answer permission prompts at the terminal, so choose how much Claude may do on its own:
  dl
    dt
      strong Default
    dd
      | Claude asks before risky actions. Unanswered requests are denied, unless you turn on
      strong Walk mode
      | for the job and answer them from the Inbox.
    dt
      strong acceptEdits
    dd Claude may edit files without asking, but still asks for commands. A good default for coding jobs with walk mode on.
    dt
      strong plan
    dd Claude only plans and changes nothing.
    dt
      strong auto
      | ,
      strong dontAsk
    dd Claude Code's more autonomous modes; see Claude Code's own documentation.
    dt
      strong bypassPermissions
    dd Claude may do anything without asking. Only use it in a throwaway folder or a worktree you are happy to lose.
  h3#launch-when 4. When to run it
  p
    | Choose
    strong Run now
    | and press
    strong Launch job
    | , or choose
    strong On a schedule
    | and press
    strong Create schedule
    | (see
    a(href="#schedules") Schedules
    | ). After launching you land on the job's page.

  h2#statuses Job statuses
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Status
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Meaning
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") queued
          td(data-part="td") Waiting for the runner to come online, or for a free slot on it.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") starting
          td(data-part="td") Sent to the runner, not yet confirmed. If not confirmed within a minute it goes back to queued.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") running
          td(data-part="td") Claude is working.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") succeeded
          td(data-part="td") Claude finished without an error.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") failed
          td(data-part="td") The runner refused it (for example a folder outside the allowed roots), or Claude ended with an error or hit a budget or turn limit. The reason is shown in red.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") stopped
          td(data-part="td") You stopped or cancelled it, or its time limit was reached.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") lost
          td(data-part="td") The runner lost contact for more than 2 minutes. If it reconnects and reports the real outcome, the status is corrected.
  p
    | A job stays
    strong running
    | for about two minutes after Claude has finished, so that you can still send it a follow-up message. It then turns
    | into
    strong succeeded
    | by itself.
  h3#queue How the queue works
  ul
    li Each machine runs at most 2 jobs at a time by default (its runner's concurrency).
    li Only one job runs per repository at a time; others for the same repository wait.
    li Queued jobs start within a few seconds of a slot freeing up.

  h2#list The job list
  p
    | The
    strong Jobs
    | page lists jobs newest first, filtered with
    strong All
    | ,
    strong Active
    | ,
    strong Done
    | and
    strong Failed
    | . Each row shows the status, title, machine, folder, cost, duration and, for jobs with a time limit, how much time
    | is left.

  h2#detail The job page
  p At the top: the status, the machine, cost against budget (for example $0.42 / $5.00), duration and remaining time (amber in the last 5 minutes), the folder, a link to its session, and a summary of its options.
  dl
    dt
      strong Cancel
      | /
      strong Stop
    dd
      | Cancels a queued job, or stops a running one. Press twice: the button changes to
      strong Tap again to stop
      | so a stray tap does nothing.
    dt
      strong Rerun
    dd Starts a new job with the same prompt and options (for ended jobs).
    dt
      strong Resume
    dd
      | Starts a new job that continues the same Claude session, with an optional message (by default "Continue where
      | you left off."). Useful after a job ran out of budget or time.
  h3#output Output
  p
    | The output shows what Claude is doing, live: its messages, thinking, tool calls (tap one to see its input), tool
    | results and a final summary such as "Result · success · $0.12 · 5 turns · 40 s". Toggle
    strong Follow
    | to stay at the bottom and
    strong Raw
    | to see the underlying lines. The newest 400 items are shown, with a button for earlier ones.
  h3#follow-up Follow-up messages
  p
    | While a job is live, type in
    strong Send a follow-up message…
    | and press
    strong Send
    | . It appears in the output as a
    strong You
    | bubble and Claude continues with it.
  h3#verify Verify
  p
    | Agents sometimes say "all tests pass" when they do not. The
    strong Verify
    | card lets the runner check for itself after the job ends: it runs the
    strong Done when
    | commands from the prompt (the ones written in backticks) in the job's folder, one by one.
  ol
    li
      | Check the list in
      strong Commands (one per line)
      | . It is pre-filled from the prompt.
    li
      | Press
      strong Verify N commands…
      | , read the confirmation ("Run these N commands with the shell in …?") and press
      strong Confirm and run
      | .
    li Each command shows PASS or FAIL with its exit code and output, and the card says how many passed.
  +callout(variant="muted")
    @content
      p
        | For safety the runner only runs commands that appeared in that job's own prompt, and only if it is the runner
        | that ran the job and it has not restarted since. Each command may take up to 10 minutes.

  h2#schedules Schedules
  p
    | A schedule launches the same prompt again and again, for example every night at 2:00. Create one from the launch
    | form: fill it in as usual, choose
    strong On a schedule
    | , type a cron expression in
    strong Cron (minute hour day month weekday, server time)
    | , and press
    strong Create schedule
    | . Schedules are listed under
    strong Schedules
    | on the Jobs page with their next and last run.
  h3#cron Writing a cron expression
  p Five fields separated by spaces: minute (0–59), hour (0–23), day of the month (1–31), month (1–12 or jan–dec) and weekday (0–7 or sun–sat, where 0 and 7 are Sunday).
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Expression
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Runs
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code 0 2 * * *
          td(data-part="td") every night at 02:00
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code 30 6 * * 1-5
          td(data-part="td") at 06:30 on weekdays
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code 0 */4 * * *
          td(data-part="td") every 4 hours, on the hour
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code 0 9 1 * *
          td(data-part="td") at 09:00 on the first of every month
  p
    code *
    | means every value; use commas for lists (
    code 1,15
    | ), a dash for ranges (
    code 1-5
    | ) and a slash for steps (
    code */15
    | ). Shortcuts such as
    code @daily
    | are not supported.
  h3#schedule-notes Good to know
  ul
    li Times are in the server's time zone.
    li Runs missed while the server was down are not made up later.
    li A schedule uses the saved prompt's current version every time it fires, so editing the prompt changes future runs.
    li
      | Schedules cannot be paused or edited. To change one, delete it (press
      strong Delete
      | , then
      strong Tap again to delete
      | ) and create it again.
